WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:05.779
In 2020, the executives at Tesla all received
a base salary of $300,000.

00:00:05.779 --> 00:00:07.470
Except for one executive.

00:00:07.470 --> 00:00:10.950
Elon Musk received a big fat $0.

00:00:10.950 --> 00:00:14.960
Getting paid nothing or next to nothing is
not new for a CEO.

00:00:14.960 --> 00:00:19.109
Mark Zuckerberg’s salary for Facebook - now
Meta - in 2020 was $1.

00:00:19.109 --> 00:00:22.519
However, their compensation plans are very
different.

00:00:22.519 --> 00:00:26.509
Musk gets paid in Tesla shares ONLY if the
company does well.

00:00:26.509 --> 00:00:27.509
Really well.

00:00:27.509 --> 00:00:32.070
Elon Musk will only get paid if Tesla reaches
certain milestones.

00:00:32.070 --> 00:00:36.250
These milestones are meant to incentivize
him to continue to lead Tesla through its

00:00:36.250 --> 00:00:37.810
next phase of growth.

00:00:37.810 --> 00:00:41.680
If Tesla achieves its goals, he gets a very
handsome sum.

00:00:41.680 --> 00:00:43.840
But if the company fails, he gets nothing.

00:00:43.840 --> 00:00:48.360
Here’s how his compensation plan agreed
to in 2018 works:

00:00:48.360 --> 00:00:53.640
Tesla’s Board of Directors awarded Musk
the option to buy 101 million Tesla shares

00:00:53.640 --> 00:00:55.120
at $70 each.

00:00:55.120 --> 00:01:00.640
They’re separated into 12 tranches totaling
12% of the issued and outstanding shares at

00:01:00.640 --> 00:01:01.900
the time of the agreement.

00:01:01.900 --> 00:01:06.630
It might help to think of each tranche as
a treasure chest with 8 million shares inside.

00:01:06.630 --> 00:01:13.560
Based on Tesla’s recent share price of $1,100,
the value of each tranche would be about $8.7

00:01:13.560 --> 00:01:14.770
billion!

00:01:14.770 --> 00:01:19.159
The key to opening each treasure chest depends
on whether Tesla can hit a certain market

00:01:19.159 --> 00:01:23.450
capitalization - the value of a company within
ten years.

00:01:23.450 --> 00:01:29.039
$100 billion for the first tranche to open
up then $150 billion, $200 billion, and so

00:01:29.039 --> 00:01:32.539
on, all the way up to $650 billion.

00:01:32.539 --> 00:01:36.380
To put things into perspective, the largest
automaker in the world at the time, Toyota,

00:01:36.380 --> 00:01:40.650
had a market cap of around $191 billion.

00:01:40.650 --> 00:01:45.420
When the Board came up with these numbers,
Tesla’s market value was only $59 billion.

00:01:45.420 --> 00:01:50.579
So these goals were exceedingly difficult
if not impossible to achieve.

00:01:50.579 --> 00:01:53.450
But Tesla did it, and in very short order.

00:01:53.450 --> 00:01:57.240
Today, its market cap is well over $1 trillion
dollars.

00:01:57.240 --> 00:02:01.899
Although the market cap conditions have been
met, Musk cannot access his big payday.

00:02:01.899 --> 00:02:05.380
Turns out, there are even more roadblocks his way.

00:02:05.380 --> 00:02:11.670
Tesla has to meet 12 of 16 additional conditions
outlined in the SEC filing related to revenue

00:02:11.670 --> 00:02:12.690
and profitability.

00:02:12.690 --> 00:02:17.250
The Board knew they set the bar extremely
high – admitting these would be “challenging

00:02:17.250 --> 00:02:19.049
hurdles” to overcome.

00:02:19.049 --> 00:02:20.530
But overcome it has.

00:02:20.530 --> 00:02:26.129
Tesla has surpassed enough milestones for
Musk to unlock 7 of 12 tranches as of this

00:02:26.129 --> 00:02:29.590
video, currently worth about $61 billion.

00:02:29.590 --> 00:02:34.010
The company has to continue growing at a rapid
pace in order for Musk to get his hands on

00:02:34.010 --> 00:02:35.010
them all.

00:02:35.010 --> 00:02:38.150
Tesla’s growth so far has already been stunning.

00:02:38.150 --> 00:02:44.520
It delivered 241,000 cars in the third quarter
of 2021- that’s 73% higher than the same

00:02:44.520 --> 00:02:46.200
quarter the year before.

00:02:46.200 --> 00:02:51.870
Tesla’s Chief Financial Officer, aka “Master
of Coin” Zachary Kirkhorn laid out the company’s

00:02:51.870 --> 00:02:55.030
ambitious future plans in the third-quarter
earnings call.

00:02:55.030 --> 00:03:03.079
You know our goal as a company here is to
grow on an average pace of 50% per year…that

00:03:03.079 --> 00:03:05.069
remains the long-term goal of the company.

00:03:05.069 --> 00:03:08.200
Overseeing these long-term goals will no doubt
be Musk.

00:03:08.200 --> 00:03:12.730
The compensation agreement stipulates that
he HAS to remain CEO or he won’t get

00:03:12.730 --> 00:03:14.580
to unlock all his stock options.

00:03:14.580 --> 00:03:19.209
The only scenario in which he’d be able
to step down as CEO and still get the grants

00:03:19.209 --> 00:03:24.220
is by serving as both Chief Product Officer
and Executive Chairman of Tesla.

00:03:24.220 --> 00:03:29.409
This compensation plan is modeled off another
from 2012 that involved 10 ambitious goals.

00:03:29.409 --> 00:03:34.290
So far, Tesla has hit nine out of 10 milestones
for that deal so he’s nearly been paid out

00:03:34.290 --> 00:03:35.290
in full.

00:03:35.290 --> 00:03:38.420
Musk is now a billionaire a few hundred times
over.

00:03:38.420 --> 00:03:42.340
And could even become the world’s first
trillionaire according to investment firm

00:03:42.340 --> 00:03:43.340
Morgan Stanley.

00:03:43.340 --> 00:03:47.020
Some have criticized the world’s richest
man for how much money he makes.

00:03:47.020 --> 00:03:52.299
Democratic senator Ron Wyden recently proposed
taxing the unrealized capital gains of the

00:03:52.299 --> 00:03:53.299
uber-wealthy.

00:03:53.299 --> 00:03:56.569
Currently, you only pay taxes when you sell
your shares.

00:03:56.569 --> 00:04:01.109
Under this plan, billionaires would be taxed
if their assets have gained value even if

00:04:01.109 --> 00:04:02.620
they haven’t sold them.

00:04:02.620 --> 00:04:07.260
In response, Musk tweeted: Eventually, they
run out of other people’s money and then

00:04:07.260 --> 00:04:08.439
they come for you.

00:04:08.439 --> 00:04:10.700
As in, the average investor.

00:04:10.700 --> 00:04:15.400
Around this time, the Tesla CEO asked his
63 million followers on Twitter whether he

00:04:15.400 --> 00:04:18.130
should sell 10% of his Tesla stock.

00:04:18.130 --> 00:04:22.680
He put it to a poll that favored selling which
is reportedly meant to help him pay his taxes

00:04:22.680 --> 00:04:25.729
on the stock options granted to him in 2012.

00:04:25.729 --> 00:04:30.500
If and when Tesla hits all of its goals which
would allow Musk to unlock all his options,

00:04:30.500 --> 00:04:32.860
what will he do with all of that money?

00:04:32.860 --> 00:04:37.260
He says half of his wealth is intended to
help with problems on Earth and half to build

00:04:37.260 --> 00:04:39.210
a city on Mars.

00:04:39.210 --> 00:04:43.700
His other company, SpaceX, is developing fully
reusable Starships to make the journey to

00:04:43.700 --> 00:04:47.830
the red planet less expensive but the costs
will still be staggering.

00:04:47.830 --> 00:04:53.120
Just like his rockets, he hopes Tesla continues
to soar to new heights, rewarding customers,

00:04:53.120 --> 00:04:56.030
employees, and shareholders along the way.

00:04:56.030 --> 00:05:00.900
And as he grows the Tesla pie - his slice
gets ever bigger, too

00:05:00.900 --> 00:05:05.919
Tesla’s stock price has already gone up
nearly 13 times its value compared to the

00:05:05.919 --> 00:05:07.660
start of 2020.

00:05:07.660 --> 00:05:11.520
If you'd rather not buy into the bull market,
there’s another way to invest.

00:05:11.520 --> 00:05:15.349
And that brings me to my sponsor Masterworks.

00:05:15.349 --> 00:05:20.520
Historically, only the super, super rich have
been able to afford a Picasso or a Warhol.

00:05:20.520 --> 00:05:23.539
That’s no longer the case.

00:05:23.539 --> 00:05:27.543
Masterworks lets you invest in multi-million-dollar
